1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,690
It's the sixteenth of
February, twenty twenty-two.

2
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:04,930
A ship is crossing the Atlantic Ocean with

3
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:07,890
almost four thousand
brand-new cars on board.

4
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:08,550
Porsches.

5
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:09,510
Bentleys.

6
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:11,490
Even a few Lamborghinis.

7
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:15,010
At eight thirty in the morning,
a smoke alarm goes off.

8
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:17,350
Two weeks later, the ship and every single

9
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:19,630
car on it are at the bottom of the ocean.

10
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:20,810
Nobody died.

11
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:25,250
But the cargo was worth hundreds of
millions of dollars, and four years later,

12
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:28,330
a court still couldn't say for
sure what started the fire.

13
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:30,550
So what happened on that ship?

14
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:33,810
To find out, we need to go back
to the morning of the alarm.

15
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:36,170
The ship was called the Felicity Ace.

16
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:39,310
It was a car carrier, about
two hundred metres long,

17
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:43,230
and from the outside it looks
like a giant floating shoebox.

18
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:46,670
Inside, it's basically
a multi-storey car park.

19
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:49,530
Eleven decks of cars,
stacked on top of each other,

20
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,210
with room for more than five thousand.

21
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,150
The cars are driven on board
up a big ramp at the back,

22
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,790
parked just centimetres apart,

23
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:00,850
and strapped to the deck so they
can't slide around in a storm.

24
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,670
On this trip, it had
loaded three thousand nine

25
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:06,930
hundred and sixty-five
vehicles in Emden, in Germany,

26
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:09,650
bound for Rhode Island,
in the United States.

27
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:11,890
About three and a half
thousand nautical miles.

28
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:13,870
Most were from the Volkswagen Group.

29
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:15,830
Around eleven hundred were Porsches.

30
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:17,630
A hundred and eighty-nine were Bentleys.

31
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:20,950
And fifteen were the
Lamborghini Aventador Ultimae,

32
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,430
the very last version of
a famous V12 supercar.

33
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:27,210
One insurance analyst
estimated the cars alone

34
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:29,690
were worth around four
hundred million dollars.

35
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:31,850
And there was one more detail.

36
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,970
Five hundred and seventy-four
of those cars were electric.

37
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:35,490
Keep that number in mind.

38
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:36,970
The trip started rough.

39
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:39,070
Three days out, the ship hit a gale,

40
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:41,570
with waves around three metres high.

41
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:44,990
The captain changed course to
stop the ship slamming into them.

42
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:46,470
Then the weather calmed down.

43
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,650
The crew did their safety rounds
through the car decks, day and night.

44
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:51,970
One in the morning.

45
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:53,130
Five in the morning.

46
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:54,430
Seven in the morning.

47
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:55,680
Nothing unusual.

48
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,830
At ten to eight, the third officer came

49
00:01:57,880 --> 00:01:59,870
up to the bridge to take over the watch.

50
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:01,350
The captain was there too.

51
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:02,510
Everything was normal.

52
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:05,150
And then, at eight thirty, the alarm.

53
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:08,210
The smoke detector said: deck
one, at the back of the ship.

54
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:10,930
And on this ship, deck
one isn't the bottom.

55
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:16,170
The car decks are counted from the top
down, so deck one is the highest car deck,

56
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:18,750
more than thirty metres
above the bottom of the hull.

57
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:21,950
From the bridge, nobody
could see any smoke.

58
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:24,450
So the officer radioed
a sailor who was already

59
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:27,250
doing a safety round and
asked him to go and check.

60
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:31,230
The sailor went down the stairs
and opened the door to deck one.

61
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:32,190
Black smoke.

62
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,970
And between the rows of cars, flames.

63
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:36,770
Three or four cars already burning.

64
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:38,350
And small explosions.

65
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:40,230
The sailor grabbed a fire extinguisher,

66
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:42,390
but couldn't get anywhere near the flames.

67
00:02:42,540 --> 00:02:43,790
The smoke was too thick.

68
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,550
So the door went shut again, and the
alarm went out to the whole ship.

69
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:51,130
The fire team got ready fast, with
breathing sets and fire suits.

70
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:52,670
They opened the door again.

71
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:54,190
They couldn't see a thing.

72
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,110
The crew started the emergency
fire pump and sprayed

73
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:00,570
water on the outside of the
deck above, to cool it down.

74
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:04,490
Then the captain went to plan B. The
ship's fixed firefighting system.

75
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:07,370
Car carriers don't use
sprinklers like a building.

76
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:09,410
This one used high-expansion foam.

77
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:10,630
The idea is simple.

78
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:12,730
A pump mixes foam liquid with water,

79
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:15,490
and big electric fans blow air through it.

80
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:18,870
That air puffs the mixture up
to hundreds of times its size,

81
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:22,490
into a huge wave of foam
that fills the whole deck and

82
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:25,710
smothers the fire, like the
world's biggest bubble bath.

83
00:03:25,860 --> 00:03:27,610
The first engineer ran to the room where

84
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:30,130
the foam system was,
and started the pumps.

85
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:31,470
Foam liquid was flowing.

86
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:33,450
The level in the tank was going down.

87
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:35,590
But the fans on deck one didn't start.

88
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:37,350
No fans, no foam.

89
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:39,630
Just liquid running out of the pipes.

90
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:41,690
The engineer ran up to the electrical room

91
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:43,910
to check the fan circuits and breakers.

92
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:45,050
Still nothing.

93
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:46,290
The fans would not start.

94
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:48,110
Meanwhile, the fire team was reporting

95
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:50,050
that the fire was out of control.

96
00:03:50,380 --> 00:03:51,950
So the captain made the call.

97
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:53,270
Abandon ship.

98
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:55,210
The ship sent out a distress call.

99
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:57,010
A tanker called the Resilient Warrior,

100
00:03:57,060 --> 00:04:00,550
on her way to Rotterdam,
answered and turned around.

101
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:03,110
Within about an hour, all twenty-two crew

102
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,630
had climbed down a rope ladder to safety.

103
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,390
Later, a Portuguese Air
Force helicopter flew

104
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,170
them to the island of
Faial, in the Azores.

105
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:12,530
Nobody was hurt.

106
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:14,970
The Felicity Ace was left on her own,

107
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:17,310
burning, in the middle of the Atlantic.

108
00:04:17,460 --> 00:04:20,050
So why was this fire so hard to stop?

109
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:22,810
First, a car deck is one giant open space.

110
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:26,110
There are no walls between the
cars to stop a fire spreading,

111
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:29,110
and air can flow along the whole deck.

112
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:32,870
Second, every car is
full of things that burn.

113
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:34,830
Fuel, plastic, foam seats, tyres.

114
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:38,510
Thousands of cars means thousands
of fires waiting to happen.

115
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:40,830
And third, the electric cars.

116
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:43,250
A lithium-ion battery that overheats can

117
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,590
go into what's called thermal runaway.

118
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:49,090
One cell heats up, sets off
the next one, and the next one,

119
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:51,710
and the battery keeps
feeding its own fire.

120
00:04:51,860 --> 00:04:54,090
It can even reignite after
it looks like it's out.

121
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:56,430
The investigators said it plainly.

122
00:04:56,620 --> 00:04:58,770
Car carriers, at the
time, were not prepared

123
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:01,570
to put out fires on
electric and hybrid cars.

124
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:04,610
There was no special equipment,
and no special procedure.

125
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:05,760
But here's the thing.

126
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:08,710
Car carrier fires didn't
start with electric cars.

127
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:12,530
Two years earlier, in June twenty
twenty, another car carrier,

128
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:16,750
the Höegh Xiamen, caught fire in the
port of Jacksonville, in Florida.

129
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:20,150
She was full of used cars,
most of them running on petrol.

130
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:23,710
America's National Transportation
Safety Board found the cause.

131
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:26,010
The batteries in some
of those used cars were

132
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:28,750
supposed to be disconnected
and secured before loading.

133
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:32,610
One wasn't done properly, and an
electrical fault started the fire.

134
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:34,830
That ship burned for more than a week.

135
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:37,830
Nine firefighters were injured,
and the ship and all two

136
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:41,130
thousand four hundred and
twenty cars were a total loss.

137
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,610
So any car carrier fire is hard.

138
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:47,270
Electric cars just add one more
problem nobody had fully solved.

139
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:49,690
The owners called in a salvage company,

140
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:51,830
Smit Salvage, from the Netherlands.

141
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:54,870
They flew a team of sixteen
experts to the Azores.

142
00:05:55,140 --> 00:05:57,790
Tugs reached the ship and
started spraying her with water,

143
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:00,030
to cool the hull from the outside.

144
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:03,470
Day after day, the fire
kept burning inside.

145
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:06,350
Little by little, the flames died down.

146
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:09,210
When it was safe enough,
a salvage team climbed on

147
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:12,230
board from a small patrol
boat to see what was left.

148
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:15,550
Then a big salvage tug called
Bear got a line on board

149
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:19,670
and started towing her toward
calmer water off the Azores.

150
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:24,210
Then, on the first of March,
she started to lean to one side.

151
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:27,910
At eleven minutes past nine in the
morning, the Felicity Ace sank.

152
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:33,530
And all three thousand nine hundred
and sixty-five cars went with her.

153
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:35,670
The tugs stayed for a few days,

154
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:37,810
watching for oil leaking from the wreck.

155
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:40,430
The operator said no new
oil spills were seen,

156
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:44,090
and after that the site was watched
by satellite and from the air.

157
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:45,320
So who paid?

158
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:46,750
Some of the people waiting for a new

159
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:49,250
Porsche or Bentley had
to wait a lot longer.

160
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:51,950
And Lamborghini did something unusual.

161
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:54,290
It had already finished
building the Aventador.

162
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:57,470
But fifteen of the last ones
were now on the sea floor.

163
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:01,130
So it restarted production, just
to build those fifteen cars again.

164
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:03,850
But the biggest question was still open.

165
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Who was to blame?

166
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:06,690
The ship flew the flag of Panama,

167
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:09,590
so Panama's maritime
authority investigated.

168
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:14,070
According to the crew, the fire
started on an electric Porsche Taycan.

169
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:17,690
But the report also said the reason
the car caught fire was unknown.

170
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:21,070
It listed possible causes,
like a faulty battery cell,

171
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:25,930
damage during assembly, or a short circuit, but it couldn't prove any of them.

172
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:28,810
The report also asked a hard question.

173
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:30,750
Could the fire have been stopped early?

174
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:34,770
A crew member had taken a photo
inside deck one, and in it,

175
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:37,650
the investigators could still
see the cars and the lights.

176
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:40,510
They thought a fire team
might have been able to go in,

177
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:42,470
use the hoses already fitted on the deck,

178
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:45,370
and stop the fire
spreading from car to car.

179
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:48,190
But they also found that
the crew had no special

180
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:51,210
training for car deck
fires, or for electric cars,

181
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:54,410
and little confidence going
into a space like that.

182
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:56,810
And the fans that never started?

183
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:59,950
The investigators thought
their motor sat on deck one,

184
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,930
near the fire, and its cables
were probably damaged by the heat.

185
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:07,390
The ship's owner and insurers
took Porsche to court in Germany,

186
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:09,810
asking for about thirty million euros.

187
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:12,570
Their argument: Porsche should
have warned them about the

188
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:16,090
risks of shipping electric cars,
and how to carry them safely.

189
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:19,970
In May twenty twenty-six, a court
in Stuttgart rejected the claim.

190
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:21,890
The judges said they were not convinced

191
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:23,950
that a Taycan had started the fire.

192
00:08:24,140 --> 00:08:25,990
That ruling can still be appealed,

193
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:28,890
and a second case in another
German court is still going.

194
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:30,890
So, four years later,

195
00:08:31,140 --> 00:08:34,210
nobody has officially proven
exactly what started it.

196
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:36,370
And this wasn't the last time.

197
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:39,290
In July twenty twenty-three,
another car carrier,

198
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:42,810
the Fremantle Highway, caught fire
off the coast of the Netherlands.

199
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:44,430
One crew member died.

200
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:47,850
Since then, Europe's maritime
safety agency has published guidance

201
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:51,550
on carrying electric cars on ships,
and the International Maritime

202
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:55,230
Organization has been working on
new fire rules for car carriers.

203
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:56,250
Better detection.

204
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:58,310
Better ways to spot a hot car early.

205
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:00,850
Better ways to fight a
fire before it spreads.

206
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:02,810
So let's go back to that morning.

207
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:04,270
The alarm goes off.

208
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:05,850
A sailor opens a door.

209
00:09:06,060 --> 00:09:06,730
Smoke.

210
00:09:06,780 --> 00:09:08,250
Three or four cars on fire.

211
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:11,250
The ship has a system built
for exactly this moment.

212
00:09:11,540 --> 00:09:13,030
Foam, pumps, fans.

213
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:15,370
But one part doesn't start.

214
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:19,067
And two weeks later, three
thousand nine hundred and

215
00:09:19,117 --> 00:09:22,550
sixty-five cars are lying
on the bottom of the ocean.

216
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:25,250
The cars on these ships are changing fast.

217
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:26,950
The ships are still catching up.

218
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:30,970
And if you want to see just how expensive
a normal day is for a ship like this,

219
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:34,510
even when nothing goes wrong,
that video is right here.
